WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
This glowing membrane looks frozen.

2
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.620
How can you see it move?

3
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.500
When every protein shines the same green, swapping places leaves the picture

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.300
unchanged. To reveal motion, you first turn one spot dark.

5
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.650
First, tag the target molecules with a fluorescent dye or GFP.

6
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.990
The tag absorbs blue light and emits green light.

7
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.690
A cell making GFP carries its own lamp while alive.

8
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.950
Next, a focused laser bleaches one small spot.

9
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:39.410
The molecules stay, but no longer glow.

10
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:45.623
Then unbleached molecules diffuse sideways into the dark spot as bleached ones

11
00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:51.203
wander out. This is fluorescence recovery after photobleaching, or FRAP.

12
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:56.943
How fast light returns gives the diffusion coefficient, a measure of spreading

13
00:00:56.943 --> 00:01:02.963
speed. How completely it returns gives the mobile fraction, the share free to move.

14
00:01:04.323 --> 00:01:07.023
Bleaching one spot turns hidden movement into a curve.

15
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:12.367
Now watch a second membrane protein after the same laser bleach.

16
00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:18.247
The dark spot brightens at first, then stalls at 50% % of its starting glow.

17
00:01:19.527 --> 00:01:22.407
What happened to the other half of those labeled molecules?

18
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:33.567
Starting from dark, mobile fraction equals recovered brightness divided by starting

19
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:40.047
brightness. Reaching half the starting glow gives 50%, so half the labeled molecules

20
00:01:40.047 --> 00:01:44.187
cannot move. Often they are anchored to the cytoskeleton.

21
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:48.287
Even full recovery can still mislead you.

22
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:53.327
FRAP measures turnover, the swapping of old molecules for new ones.

23
00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:59.427
Free diffusion gives turnover, but binding and unbinding at fixed sites give it too.

24
00:02:00.807 --> 00:02:04.327
Bleached molecules let go, and glowing ones bind the empty sites.

25
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.980
Some large nuclear bodies may be droplets, a separate liquid phase.

26
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.820
Half-body FRAP tests this.

27
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.280
Bleach one half; track both halves and the fluid around them.

28
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.600
A droplet has a boundary, so mixing inside should beat crossing its edge.

29
00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.060
That favours a phase without proving one.

30
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.920
Weak edges may slow crossing only a little, and rebinding in clusters mixes

31
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.040
internally too.

32
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:37.093
Photobleaching has also tested cell barriers.

33
00:02:38.593 --> 00:02:44.033
Textbooks once proposed a sharp 40 kDa limit for passive passage through nuclear

34
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:50.793
pores. In 2016, Timney and colleagues bleached cytoplasmic fluorescence from

35
00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:53.393
different-sized reporters in live budding yeast.

36
00:02:54.853 --> 00:02:58.253
Glowing reporters left in the nucleus leaked out through the pores.

37
00:02:59.633 --> 00:03:04.433
Their excess signal relaxed toward equilibrium, more slowly for larger reporters,

38
00:03:04.833 --> 00:03:05.973
with no sharp cutoff.

39
00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:13.217
In 2019, Dawei Yan and colleagues studied plasmodesmata, pores joining plant cells,

40
00:03:13.537 --> 00:03:15.317
in an Arabidopsis root mutant.

41
00:03:16.657 --> 00:03:21.597
At one interface they saw no pores with a visible cytoplasmic sleeve, the open

42
00:03:21.597 --> 00:03:27.937
channel inside. Yet FRAP showed GFP crossing faster than in wild-type roots.

43
00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:33.557
Erasing one patch of light turns hidden exchange into motion you can watch.
